INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('a0a34871-f5a8-4b72-a907-5352a6bdb036', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 635.18, 695.28, 'It''s actually an interesting suggestion, which is going to anticipate what''s happening later in this lecture, which was to sort the set and then binary search. Right? But let''s say that actually I only have to do this once. For some reason, I build up a whole set of the people in this classroom. And I just want to know, is Eric Demain in this class? Right? So then that algorithm would take n log n time, right? Because I''ve got to sort everybody. And then I have to do binary search, which is maybe log n time. But I claim that if the only thing I care about is building up my entire set and searching it once, there''s actually a faster algorithm. This is going to be needlessly confusing, because we''re going to see that this is really not the right way to implement it in about 38 seconds. Yes? Yeah, just iterate from beginning to this array and say, is this guy Eric? No. Is this guy Eric? No. Is this guy Eric? Yes. And then return it. Right? So in the worst case, how long will that algorithm take? Well, in the worst case, I have really bad luck. And your instructor is all the way at the end of the list. Right? So in this case, what is that going to mean?', 'in key order
smallest key
largest key
smallest key larger than k
largest key smaller than k

