INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('3fe7af34-009f-454d-b451-cf6ad17b6c82', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 884.06, 944.94, 'Right? So in other words, if I have this array of all of the students in our classroom, and the very first element in my array is going to be the student with the smallest ID number. The second is the second smallest ID number, all the way to the end of the array, which is the student with the biggest ID number. Now, does that mean I want to do arithmetic on student ID numbers? Absolutely not. But it''s just a way to sort of impose order on that list so that I can search it very quickly later. OK. So if I want to fill in the set interface, and I have somehow a sorted array of students, right? So again, they''re organized by student ID number. Then my runtime starts to get a little more interesting. Yeah? So now insertion, deletion still take the same amount of time. But let''s say that I want to find the student with the minimum ID number, right? This find min function. Well, how could I do it in a sorted array? Keyword is sorted here. Where''s the min element of an array? Yes? Yeah.', 'in key order
smallest key
largest key
smallest key larger than k
largest key smaller than k

