INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('8c4a9cf3-842c-44cb-beee-07564c1e791f', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 1196.74, 1263.7, 'Two vocabulary words I''m going to highlight really quick. One is if your sort is destructive, what that means is that rather than reserving some new memory for my sorted array B and then putting a sorted version of A into B, a destructive algorithm is one that just overwrites A with a sorted version of A. So a lot of like, certainly the C++ interface does about this. I assume the Python one does too. I always forget this detail. In addition to destructive sorts, some sorts are in place, meaning that not only are they destructive, but they also don''t use extra memory in the process of sorting. Really, you could imagine a sorting algorithm that has to reserve a bunch of scratch space to do its work and then put it back into A. For instance, the world''s dumbest destructive sort might be to call your non-destructive sort and then copy it back into A. But that would require order and space to do. So if my algorithm additionally has the property that it doesn''t reserve any extra space, at least up to a constant, then we call that in place.', 'in key order
smallest key
largest key
smallest key larger than k
largest key smaller than k

