INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('4280931d-0eee-44f7-880c-1d5911e81a2a', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 1263.7, 1324.7, 'OK. So those are our basic vocabulary words, and they''re ways to kind of understand the differences between different sorting algorithms. Yes? Why do they end up using extra O1 space? Oh, yeah, sure. Like, any time I just make a temporary variable, like a loop counter, that''s going to count to that order one. But the important thing is that the number of variables I need doesn''t scale on the length of the list. Yeah? OK. So I present to you the beginning and end of our sorting lecture, which is the world''s simplest sorting algorithm. I call it permutation sort. I think it''s very easy to prove correctness for this particular technique. So in permutation sort, what can I do? Well, I know that if I have an input that''s a list of numbers, there exists a permutation of that list of numbers that is sorted. Kind of by definition, right? Because a sort is a permutation of your original list. So what''s a very simple sorting algorithm? Well, list every possible permutation. Well, list every possible permutation.', 'in key order
smallest key
largest key
smallest key larger than k
largest key smaller than k

