INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('a7422bf7-46d8-4f19-b637-3cdd7d5c3314', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 2451, 2515, 'So life is pretty good. Unfortunately, I promised for you guys on the slides that sorting really takes n log n time. And this is an order n squared algorithm. So we''re not quite done yet. I''m way over time. So we''re going to skip a different algorithm, which is called insertion sort. Also runs in n time. OK. Where essentially insertion sort kind of runs in the reverse order. I''m going to sort everything to the left and then insert a new object. Whereas in selection sort, I''m going to choose the biggest object and then sort everything to the left. But I''ll let you guys piece through that at home. It''s essentially the same argument. And instead, we should jump to an algorithm that actually matters, which is something called merge sort. How many of you have encountered merge sort before? Fabulous. Good. So then I''m done. OK. So let''s say that I have a list. Now I''m sending a message back to Jason. I made this one up last night. OK. So I have 71562493. This is not in sorted order. But I can make a very deep observation, which is that every number by itself is in sorted order, if I think of it as an array of length one.', 'in key order
smallest key
largest key
smallest key larger than k
largest key smaller than k

