INSERT INTO public.segments (id, lecture_id, start_s, end_s, transcript, slide_text, embedding) VALUES ('016d13a0-91c4-4e8b-8d5e-3b0cbf5e6e82', '9736166c-3585-4b19-ac9b-48b30e3242ac', 2942, 3002, 'There''s an if statement with two pieces. But both of those pieces call merge with one fewer piece in it, right? So in some sense, we have S of n equals S n minus 1 plus theta of 1, which we already know from our previous proof means that S of n is equal to theta of n. So in other words, it takes linear time to merge. It kind of makes sense intuitively, right? Because essentially, you''re touching every one of these things once with your two fingers. And now, probably the hardest part of the lecture, which I left zero time for, is deriving the runtime for the actual merge sort algorithm. And what does that look like? Well, that one''s a little bit trickier, right? Because, of course, I call the merge sort algorithm twice, each time on a list that''s half the size. In this class, we''re going to assume that our list is always the power of 2 in its length.', 'in key order
